<section xml:id="sect-addition-subtraction">
  <title>Addition and Subtraction</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Addition is the mathematical operation that combines two numbers, called the <alert>summands</alert> or <alert>addends</alert>.
      The result is called the <alert>sum</alert>.
      For example,
      <me>
        \underbrace{3}_{\text{addend}}+ \underbrace{5}_{\text{addend}}= \underbrace{8}_{\text{sum}}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      A number line shows this quite nicely.
      If one starts at 3 and makes 5 hops to the right, results in 8 as shown:
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-number-line-addition">
      <caption></caption>
      <image>
        <shortdescription>
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[ 
              \begin{tikzpicture}[decoration=brace]
                \draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (10.5,0);
                \foreach \x in {0,1,2,...,10} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node [below] {\x};}
                \foreach \x in {3,4,...,7} {\draw[<-,thick] ({\x+1},0.2) .. controls ({\x+0.5},0.45) .. (\x,0.2); }
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Set Model </title>

      <p>
        Addition in the set model usually entails if there are two collections of objects and one wants the total number of objects.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            You have 8 apples and your friend has 5 apples.
            How many apples do you have together?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            The answer is the sum and a model of this could be
          </p>

          <image>
            <shortdescription>
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[ 
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \foreach \x in {0,1.5,3,4.5,7.75,9.25} \draw (\x,0) pic[scale=0.5] {apple};
                    \foreach \x in {0,1.5,3,4.5,7,8.5,10} \draw (\x,1.5) pic[scale=0.5] {apple};
                    \draw[dashed,rounded corners, thin] (-0.75,-1) rectangle (5.5,2.25);
                    \draw[dashed,rounded corners, thin] (6.25,-1) rectangle (10.75,2.25);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>

          <p>
            But of course, as the student's abstraction increases, using circles or stars would be fine as well.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Measurement Model </title>

      <p>
        Addition in the measurement model involves two measurements and you want to know the total measurement of the two.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A recipe for cookies calls for 4 cups of flour.
            A recipe for cake calls for 3 cups of flour.
            How much flour do you need to for both recipes?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[ 
            \begin{tikzpicture}[decoration=brace]
              \draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (10.5,0);
              \foreach \x in {0,1,2,...,10} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node [below] {\x};}
              \foreach \x in {4,5,6} {\draw[->,thick] ({\x},0.2) .. controls ({\x+0.5},0.45) .. ({\x+1},0.2); }
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>

          <p>
            and the result is 7, so you would need 7 cups of flour in total.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
    </paragraphs>
  </introduction>


  <subsection xml:id="sect-add-props">
    <title>Properties of Addition</title>

    <p>
      There are three properties of addition that you probably <em>know</em> just by understanding addition.
    </p>

    <axiom xml:id="prop-addition">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For any whole numbers, <m>a, b, c</m>, each of the following hold:
        </p>

        <p>
          <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Additive Identity</title>

            <p>
              <me>
                a+0=a
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Commutative Property</title>

            <p>
              <me>
                a+b = b+a
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Associative Property</title>

            <p>
              <me>
                (a+b) + c = a + (b+c)
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </axiom>

    <p>
      The additive identity property shows that adding 0 to any number doesn't change the number.
      This make a lot of sense after understanding the concept of 0.
      The commutative property shows that the sum of two numbers is independent of the order of the two numbers.
      The associative property relies on recalling that when parentheses are present, that one must do the operation inside the parentheses first.
      This shows if you add the first two then add the result to the 3, the sum is the same as adding the last two, then adding to the first.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What property is being used in each of the following?
        </p>

        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>(6+2)+4 = (2+6) + 4</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>(2+6)+4 = 2+(6+4)</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>7+0=7</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>

        <p />
          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  This is the <em>commutative property</em>.
                  Notice that there are three numbers, but the first two swap places.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  This is the <em>associative property</em>.
                  Notice that we have changed the order of operations.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  This is the additive identity.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <lemma xml:id="lem-any-order-add">
        <title>Any-Order Property of Addition</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            The sum <m>a+b+c</m> can be done in any order.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </lemma>

      <p>
        We won't present a formal proof here, but this is a consequence of the the commutative and associative properties.
      </p>

      <corollary xml:id="cor-extended-any-order-add">
        <title>Extended Any-Order Property</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            The sum of any number of addends can be done in any order.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </corollary>

      <p>
        Again, no formal proof will be done here but this is a consequence of a number of applications of the commutative and associative properties.
        The following show an example.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The numbers: <m>3+2+4+7</m> can be added in any order.
            For example:
            <me>
              \begin{aligned} (3+2)+(4+7) \amp = 5 + 11 = 16\\ (7+3) + (2+4) \amp = 10 + 6 = 16\\ (3+4) + (2+7) \amp = 7 + 9 = 16 \end{aligned}
            </me>
            are all valid ways to add the 4 numbers.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <p>
        In Chapter <xref ref="ch-thinking-strategies" text="global" />, we will use this property to our advantage to rearrange the sum of numbers to pair up number that we call <em>compatible</em> in which the result is “nice.”
      </p>
    </subsection>


    <subsection xml:id="sect-add-strategies">
      <title>Thinking Strategies for Addition</title>

      <p>
        It is a good skill to have different ways of thinking about mathematical operations.
        In this section we present some ways to think about addition.
      </p>


      <paragraphs>
        <title>Number Bonds</title>

        <p>
          A number bond is useful for showing the relationship between two addends (the ends) and the sum (in the center).
          The following shows <m>7=3+4</m>.
        </p>

        <image width="20%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \draw (0,1) node[draw,rectangle,fill=MaterialBlue300]  (A) {7};
                  \draw (1,2) node[draw,rectangle,fill=MaterialBlue300]  (B) {3};
                  \draw (1,0) node[draw,rectangle,fill=MaterialBlue300]  (C) {4};
                  \draw [-] (A)--(B);
                  \draw [-] (A)--(C);
                \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          This is a nice way to visualize addition and we will see this below with four-fact families.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>


      <paragraphs>
        <title>Counting On</title>

        <p>
          When beginning to add, like <m>5+2</m>, the answer is easily found by starting at the number 5 (in the counting sequence), then counting two more, ”6,7” arriving at the answer 7.
          This can be done with young students using fingers, but also works well with the number line, like:
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75]
                  \draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (10.5,0);
                  \foreach \x in {0,1,2,...,10} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node [below] {\x};}
                  \foreach \x in {5,6} {\draw[->,thick] (\x,0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.25},0.25) and ({\x+0.75},0.25) .. ({\x+1},0.1); }
                \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          This works fine with adding 1,2,3, just a few to a number, but can be extended by counting by tens as well.
          Consider <m>13 + 24</m>.
          We can start at <m>13</m>, add two 10s to get to 33, then add 4 to get to 37.
          This can be seen with the following number line:
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                  \draw[->] (-0.1,0) -- (4.5,0);
                  \foreach \x in {0,10,13,20,23,30,33,40} {\draw ({0.1*\x},0.1) -- ({0.1*\x},-0.1) node [below] {\x};}
                  \foreach \x in {1,...,40} \draw ({\x/10},0.05) -- ({\x/10},-0.05); 
                  %\draw[<-] (3,0.2) .. controls (5,0.75) and (6,0.75) .. (8,0.2) node[midway, above] {?};  
                  \foreach \x in {1.3,2.3} {\draw[->,thick] (\x,0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.25},0.25) and ({\x+0.75},0.25) .. ({\x+1},0.1); }
                  \foreach \x in {3.3,3.4,3.5,3.6} {\draw[->,thick] (\x,0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.025},0.15) and ({\x+0.075},0.15) .. ({\x+0.1},0.1); }
                \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          And from the number line, you can see the result is 37.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>


      <paragraphs>
        <title>Tens Combination</title>

        <p>
          Two numbers whose sum is 10 is crucial to do arithmetic operations at all levels.
          For example, since <m>4+6=10</m> the numbers 4 and 6 are a <term>tens combination</term> and is shown in the number bond
        </p>

        <image width="20%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \draw (0,1) node[draw,rectangle,fill=MaterialBlue300]  (A) {10};
                  \draw (1,2) node[draw,rectangle,fill=MaterialBlue300]  (B) {6};
                  \draw (1,0) node[draw,rectangle,fill=MaterialBlue300]  (C) {4};
                  \draw [-] (A)--(B);
                  \draw [-] (A)--(C);
                \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          How many pairs are there that add to 10?
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsection>


    <subsection>
      <title>Addition and Place Value</title>

      <p>
        There are multiple approaches to understanding addition and place value.
        First, using the expanded form of a number, then a chip model and finally the DLB model.
      </p>


      <paragraphs>
        <title>Expanded Form:</title>

        <p>
          As seen above, an addition like <m>13+24</m> is relatively straightforward once a student understands place value.
          If we used expanded form on these:
        </p>

        <p>
          <me>
            \begin{aligned} 13+24\amp= (10+3)+(20+4) \qquad\text{use any-order property}\\ \amp= (10+20) + (3+4) \\ \amp= 30 + 7 = 37\end{aligned}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          This gets a bit more more complicated when the total number of ones (pennies) exceeds 9.
          For example, <m>25 + 37</m> can be written as
          <me>
            \begin{aligned} 25 + 37\amp= (20 + 5) + (30 + 7) \qquad \text{ Use the any-order property,}\\ \amp= (20 + 30) + (5+7) \\ \amp= 50 + 12 \\ \amp= 50 + 10 + 2 \\ \amp= 60 + 2 = 62 \end{aligned}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          The writing of <m>5+7=12=10+2</m> is called <term>composing a ten</term> or <term>bundling</term> as we will see with models below.<fn>Notice the way that we write these operations.
          We start with the original problem, and link each step with an equals sign.
          This is an important skill to develop and we will work on this in Chapter \html-tag:xref{\html-attr:ref{"ch:mental:math"}\html-attr:text{"global"}}.
          Until we get there, pay attention to the steps and how they are written.
          </fn>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>


      <paragraphs>
        <title>Coin Model </title>

        <p>
          We can use a coin model to find <m>13+24</m> as
        </p>

        <image>
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[ 
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw (0,0) node[draw, circle] {10};
                \foreach \x in {-1,0,1} \draw (\x,1) node[draw, circle] {1}; 
                \foreach \x in {5,6} \draw(\x,0) node [draw,circle] {10}; 
                \foreach \x in {4,5,6,7} \draw(\x,1) node [draw,circle] {1};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          which can be interpreted as 1 dime (10 cents) and 3 pennies (each 1 cent) plus 2 dimes and 4 pennies.
          The result is 3 dimes and 7 pennies or 37¢.
        </p>

        <p>
          Again, as we saw above, within one denomination, once you exceed 9, you must bundle.
          The coin model for <m>25+37</m> is:
        </p>

        <image>
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
            \begin{tikzpicture}
              \foreach \x in {0,1} \draw (\x,0) node[draw, circle] {10};
              \foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} \draw (\x,1) node[draw, circle] {1}; 
              \foreach \x in {6,7,8} \draw(\x,0) node [draw,circle] {10}; 
              \foreach \x in {4,...,10} \draw(\x,1) node [draw,circle] {1};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          The bundling occurs when we take 10 pennies and exchange them for a dime.
          That is we \emph{bundle} 10 pennies for a higher denomination coin (a dime).
        </p>

        <image>
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \foreach \x in {0,1} \draw (\x,0) node[draw, circle] {10};
            \foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} \draw (\x,1) node[draw, circle] {1}; 
            \foreach \x in {6,7,8} \draw(\x,0) node [draw,circle] {10}; 
            \foreach \x in {4,...,10} \draw(\x,1) node [draw,circle] {1};
            \draw[rounded corners] (-0.5,0.5) rectangle (10.5,1.5);
            \foreach \x in {0,1,2,4,5,...,10} \draw[thick,MaterialIndigo] ({\x-0.4},0.6) -- ({\x+0.4},1.4); 
            \draw[->,thick] (9,0.6) -- (9.4,0.3);
            \draw (9.7,-0.2) node[draw, circle] {10}; 
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          And it should be clear to see that what is left (not crossed out) is 6 dimes and 2 pennies or 62.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>


      <paragraphs>
        <title>Dots Lines and Boxes (DLB) Model </title>

        <p>
          Alternatively, we can use the DLB (dots, lines and boxes) model seen above.
          We can write 25 as two lines and 5 dots and 37 as 3 lines and 7 dots.
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
            \begin{tikzpicture}
              \foreach \x in {0,0.2} \draw[thick] (\x,0)--(\x,1);
              \foreach \y in {0.1,0.3,...,0.9} \fill (0.5,\y) circle[radius=2pt];  
              \foreach \x in {2,2.2,2.4} \draw[thick] (\x,0)--(\x,1);
              \foreach \y in {0.1,0.3,...,0.9} \fill (2.6,\y) circle[radius=2pt];
              \foreach \y in {0.1,0.3} \fill (2.8,\y) circle[radius=2pt];  
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          and we can rearrange the lines and dots to get:
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \foreach \x in {0,0.2,...,0.8} \draw[thick] (\x,0)--(\x,1);
            \foreach \y in {0.1,0.3,...,0.9} \fill (1.2,\y) circle[radius=2pt];
            \foreach \y in {0.1,0.3,...,0.9} \fill (1.4,\y) circle[radius=2pt];  
            \foreach \y in {0.1,0.3} \fill (2,\y) circle[radius=2pt];
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          and in this case, we bundle ten dots to get a line or
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
            \begin{tikzpicture}
              \foreach \x in {0,0.2,...,1} \draw[thick] (\x,0)--(\x,1);
              \foreach \y in {0.1,0.3} \fill (2,\y) circle[radius=2pt];
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          which again shows 6 lines and 2 dots or 62.
        </p>

        <p>
          These two models are equivalent, however crossing out and bundling is hard to see visually with the DLB model, however they are equivalent.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsection>


    <subsection>
      <title>Subtraction</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          We now turn to subtraction, which is closely tied to addition.
        </p>

        <definition>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Subtraction is defined as a missing addend.
              That is
              <me>
                c = a-b
              </me>
              is the number <m>c</m> such that <m>a = b+ c</m>.
              The number <m>a</m> is called the <alert>minuend</alert>, the number <m>b</m> is the <alert>subtrahend</alert> and the number <m>c</m> is called the <alert>difference</alert>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
          As an example, the number <m>12-7</m> is the number that fits the box:
          <me>
            7 + \boxed{\; \rule{0pt}{9pt}} =12
          </me>
          or 5.
          Using the terminology above:
          <me>
            \underbrace{12}_{\text{minuend}}- \underbrace{7}_{\text{subtrahend}} = \underbrace{5}_{\text{difference}}
          </me>
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Subtraction Interpretations:</title>

        <p>
          There are a few standard diagrams associated with subtractions, however we need to define some interpretations before diagrams.
          Subtraction is used in many ways to solve different types of problems and it is important to understand all of the types with examples.
          The following show the standard 3 ways of interpreting subtraction with examples from both set and measurement models.
        </p>


        <paragraphs>
          <title>Part-Whole Interpretation</title>

          <p>
            A part of a set or quantity is specified and we want to know how much is needed to make it whole.
            Find the missing part.
          </p>

          <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
              <title>Set Model</title>

              <p>
                There are 12 pieces of fruit (apples and oranges).
                If 5 are apples, then how many are oranges?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <title>Measurement Model </title>

              <p>
                It takes 19 miles to drive to school.
                After 7 miles, you stop for gas.
                How many miles are left in your trip?
              </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
          </p>
        </paragraphs>


        <paragraphs>
          <title>Takeaway Interpretation</title>

          <p>
            Remove objects from a set or decrease the measurement by a specified amount.
          </p>

          <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
              <title>Set Model</title>

              <p>
                Eve has a basket of 15 pieces of candy.
                She gives 6 pieces of candy to a friend.
                How many pieces of candy does Eve have left?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <title>Measurement Model </title>

              <p>
                There are 10 gallons of punch for a party.
                The guests drank 8 gallons of punch.
                How many gallons of punch are left?
              </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
          </p>
        </paragraphs>


        <paragraphs>
          <title>Comparison Interpretation</title>

          <p>
            Compare two different sets or measurements and ask how much more or less or fewer is one than the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
              <title>Set Model</title>

              <p>
                Zoë has 8 balls and Abby has 5 balls.
                How many more balls does Zoë have than Abby?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <title>Measurement Model </title>

              <p>
                José is 56 inches tall and Julia is 53 inches tall.
                How much taller is José?
              </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
          </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <p>
          For each of these the answer is the difference between the larger and smaller numbers.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Subtraction Diagrams for Set Models</title>

        <p>
          Using diagrams to understand subtraction is quite helpful.
          We first start with set model versions of the three interpretations above:
        </p>

        <p>
          <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Part-Whole</title>

            <p>
              For the part-whole interpretation, we can draw the two different objects.
              The following shows <m>13-5</m>.
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[ 
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \foreach \x in {0,1.5,3,4.5} \draw (\x,0) pic[scale=0.5] {orange}; 
                    \foreach \x in {7.75,9.25} \draw (\x,0) pic[scale=0.5] {apple};
                    \foreach \x in {0,1.5,3,4.5} \draw (\x,1.5) pic[scale=0.5] {orange};
                    \foreach \x in {7,8.5,10} \draw (\x,1.5) pic[scale=0.5] {apple}; 
                    %\draw[rounded corners, thin] (-0.75,-0.75) rectangle (5.5,2.5);
                    %\draw[rounded corners, thin] (6.25,-0.75) rectangle (10.75,2.5);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              This is a bit hard to envision for part-whole because you need to draw the minuend first and then rest you need to calculate, which is not in the spirit of diagrams.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Takeaway</title>

            <p>
              For the take-way interpretation, we draw the total number of objects (minuend) and cross out the subtrahend.
              An example of <m>12-5</m> is
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[ 
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \foreach \x in {0,1.5,...,8.5} \draw (\x,0) pic[scale=0.5] {lemon}; 
                      \foreach \x in {0,1.5,...,8.5} \draw (\x,1.5) pic[scale=0.5] {lemon}; 
                      \foreach \x in {0,1.5,...,7} \draw[very thick] ({\x-0.5},0.5) -- ({\x+0.5},-0.5); 
                      \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              And note that the difference (answer) is the number of objects that are not crossed out.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Comparison</title>

            <p>
              For the comparison interpretation, you need to be careful to ensure that the diagram shows “how many more”.
              Here’s another example with fruit showing <m>9-6</m>, which would be <em>how many more cherries than apples do you have?</em>
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \foreach \x in {0,1.5,...,13} \draw (\x,1.5) pic[scale=0.5] {cherry}; 
                  \foreach \x in {0,1.5,...,8} \draw (\x,0) pic[scale=0.5] {apple}; 
                  \draw[rounded corners] (8.5,0.9) rectangle (12.5,2.4);
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Subtraction Diagrams for Measurement Models</title>

        <p>
          Since measurement model is applicable to problems with non-integer numbers, we'll use appropriate diagrams for such numbers.
          This includes number lines and bar diagrams.
          The following are such model for the three subtraction interpretations.
        </p>

        <p>
          <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Part-Whole</title>

            <p>
              <m>8-3</m>. As an example, consider the example: <em>Alice has 8 total pounds of sugar (white and brown). 3 pounds of that are brown sugar. How much is white sugar? </em>
            </p>

            <p>
              Recall that this means the “whole” is 8 and the part is 3 and we are looking for the remainder.
              We can draw this with the following:
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (10.5,0);
                  \foreach \x in {0,1,2,...,10} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node [below] {\x};}
                  \filldraw[fill=MaterialIndigo,draw=black] (0,0.3) rectangle (3,1); 
                  \filldraw[fill=MaterialAmber,draw=black] (3,0.3) rectangle (8,1); 
                  \draw[brace] (3,1.2) -- (8,1.2) node [bracelabel=above] {?}; 
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              Alternatively, you one can just use the bar diagram without the number line as:
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \filldraw[fill=MaterialIndigo,draw=black] (0,0.3) rectangle (3,1); 
                  \filldraw[fill=MaterialAmber,draw=black] (3,0.3) rectangle (8,1); 
                  \draw[brace] (3,1.2) -- (8,1.2) node [bracelabel=above] {?}; 
                  \draw[brace] (0,1.2) -- (3,1.2) node [bracelabel=above] {3};
                  \draw[brace] (8,0.1) -- (0,0.1) node [bracelabel=below] {8}; 
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Takeaway</title>

            <p>
              The operation <m>8-3</m> with the take-away interpretation can be thought of starting with the whole (as measurement) and remove the minuend.
              Consider the example: <em>If you have a rope of length 8 meters and cut off a piece 3 meters in length, what is the remaining part?</em>
            </p>

            <p>
              We can do this on a number line with
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[decoration=brace]
                  \draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (10.5,0);
                  \foreach \x in {0,1,2,...,10} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node [below] {\x};}
                  \draw[<-,thick] (5,0.2) .. controls (6,0.75) and (7,0.75) .. (8,0.2) node[midway, above] {3};  
                  %\foreach \x in {3,4,...,7} {\draw[->] ({\x+1},0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.5},0.25) .. (\x,0.1); }
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              and the number that you land on is 5 (the difference).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Comparison</title>

            <p>
              Again, we will show <m>8-3</m>.
              For comparison, two different measurements are given and we are asked how much larger, longer, smaller, etc.
              one is than the other.
              Consider the example: <em>Joey's cat weighs 8 pounds and Jill's kitten weight 3 pounds.
              How much more does Joey's cat weigh than Jill's?</em>.
            </p>

            <p>
              Stacked bar diagrams work quite well for these, with the two quantities as the length of the two bars:
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \filldraw[fill=MaterialGreen600,draw=black] (0,1) rectangle (8,1.5); 
                  \filldraw[fill=MaterialGreen300,draw=black] (0,0) rectangle (3,0.5); 
                  \draw[brace] (0,1.6) -- (8,1.6) node[bracelabel=above] {8};
                  \draw[brace] (3,-0.1) -- (0,-0.1) node[bracelabel = below] {3};
                  \draw[brace] (8,-0.1) -- (3,-0.1) node[bracelabel = below] {?};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Thinking Strategies</title>

        <p>
          We consider some basic thinking strategies when performing subtraction.
          Many of these fall into the realm of mental math, in which one does the operation in one's head.
        </p>

        <p>
          <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Counting Down</title>

            <p>
              If the subtrahend is a small number, then counting down is an effective way to calculate a subtraction.
              For example, <m>7-2</m> starts at the number 7, counts backwards two spots to arrive at 5.
              One can think of counting down on fingers or better yet, on a number line, like
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[decoration=brace]
                  \draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (10.5,0);
                  \foreach \x in {0,1,2,...,10} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node [below] {\x};}
                  %\draw[<-] (3,0.2) .. controls (5,0.75) and (6,0.75) .. (8,0.2) node[midway, above] {?};  
                  \foreach \x in {5,6} {\draw[<-,thick] (\x,0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.25},0.25) and ({\x+0.75},0.25) .. ({\x+1},0.1); }
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              When the numbers get larger (especially the subtrahend),  there are some additional strategies.
              Consider <m>58-24</m>.
              Instead of counting down 24 individual numbers, one can first count down by two 10s and then count down by 4 ones.
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=3]
                  \draw[->] (2.5,0) -- (6.5,0);
                  \foreach \x in {30,38,40,50,58,60} {\draw ({0.1*\x},0.1) -- ({0.1*\x},-0.1) node [below] {\x};}
                  \foreach \x in {30,...,60} \draw ({\x/10},0.05) -- ({\x/10},-0.05); 
                  %\draw[<-] (3,0.2) .. controls (5,0.75) and (6,0.75) .. (8,0.2) node[midway, above] {?};  
                  \foreach \x in {4.8,3.8} {\draw[<-,thick] (\x,0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.25},0.25) and ({\x+0.75},0.25) .. ({\x+1},0.1); }
                  \foreach \x in {3.4,3.5,3.6,3.7} {\draw[<-,thick] (\x,0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.025},0.15) and ({\x+0.075},0.15) .. ({\x+0.1},0.1); }
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              Resulting in 34 for the answer.
              This is also helps with the introduction of place value and chip models.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Counting Up</title>

            <p>
              When the subtrahend is larger but close in value to the minuend, \emph{counting up} is a better strategy.
              For example, <m>17-14</m> can be found by starting at 14 and counting up to 17, with the result of 3 steps.
              is to count up from 14 to 17 (for 3), the answer
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[decoration=brace]
                  \draw[->] (9.5,0) -- (20.5,0);
                  \foreach \x in {10,...,20} \draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node [below] {\x};
                  %\draw[<-] (3,0.2) .. controls (5,0.75) and (6,0.75) .. (8,0.2) node[midway, above] {?};  
                  \foreach \x in {14,15,16} \draw[->,thick] (\x,0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.25},0.25) and ({\x+0.75},0.25) .. ({\x+1},0.1); 
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              Similar to counting down, if we have a problem like <m>27-15</m>, we start at 15 and count up to 27.
              However, first count up by 10 to 25, then up 2 to 27.
              The answer is 12.
              Visually, we can see this with:
            </p>

            <image>
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=3]
                  \draw[->] (-0.1,0) -- (3.1,0);
                  \foreach \x in {0,10,15,20,25,30} {\draw ({0.1*\x},0.05) -- ({0.1*\x},-0.05) node [below] {\x};}
                  \foreach \x in {0,...,30} \draw ({\x/10},0.02) -- ({\x/10},-0.02); 
                  \foreach \x in {1.5} {\draw[->,thick] (\x,0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.25},0.25) and ({\x+0.75},0.25) .. ({\x+1},0.1); }
                  \foreach \x in {2.5,2.6} {\draw[->,thick] (\x,0.1) .. controls ({\x+0.025},0.15) and ({\x+0.075},0.15) .. ({\x+0.1},0.1); }
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Four-Fact Families</title>

            <p>
              Combining the relationship between addition, subtraction and the commutative property of addition, results in what are termed <term>four-fact families</term>.
              For example,
              <me>
                \begin{aligned} 3+5 \amp = 8 \amp 8-3 \amp = 5 \\ 5 + 3 \amp = 8 \amp 8-5 \amp = 3 \end{aligned}
              </me>
            </p>

            <p>
              and the number bond that shows all 4 of these:
            </p>

            <image width="30%">
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
\draw (0,1) node [draw,rectangle,fill=MaterialBlue300](A) {8};
\draw (1,2) node [draw,rectangle,fill=MaterialBlue300] (B) {3};
\draw (1,0) node [draw,rectangle,fill=MaterialBlue300] (C) {5};
\draw [-] (A)--(B);
\draw [-] (A)--(C);
\end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Subtraction and Place Value</title>

        <p>
          Although we just saw that compensation can often make subtraction problems easier, in general with larger numbers, we need to understand how place value works with subtraction.
          Some problems, like <m>67 - 42</m> is straightforward in that if we write this in expanded form as
          <me>
            67 - 42 = (60 + 7) - (40 + 2) = (60-40) + (7-2) = 20 + 5 = 25
          </me>
          where basically the tens are subtracted and the ones are subtracted.
          However, consider the example <m>63 - 37</m> doesn't work so well because the ones results in a negative number.
          In this case, we need to <alert>decompose a ten</alert> like:
        </p>

        <p>
          <me>
            \begin{aligned} 63 - 37\amp= (60 + 3) - (30 + 7)\amp\amp\text{\color{MaterialIndigo} write $60 = 50 + 10$}\\\amp= (50 + 10 + 3) - (30 + 7) \\\amp= (50 + 13) - (30 +7) \\\amp= (50 - 30) + (13 -7) \\\amp= 20 + 6 = 26\end{aligned}
          </me>
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Subtraction with the chip/coin model</title>

        <p>
          We will see <m>63-37</m> in a chip/coin model as a take-away model in that the minuend is drawn as coins/chips:
        </p>

        <image>
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \foreach \x in {1,...,6} \draw (\x,1) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {\small 10};
                  \foreach \x in {1,2,3} \draw (\x,0) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {\small 1}; 
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          and we'd like take-away 3 tens and 7 ones, however, there are not each ones.
          To show decomposing a ten or <alert>unbundling</alert> a 10, cross out one 10 and write 10 ones:
        </p>

        <image>
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                  \foreach \x in {1,...,6} \draw (\x,1) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {\small 10};
                  \foreach \x in {1,2,3} \draw (\x,0) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {\small 1}; 
                  \draw[thick] (5.6,0.6) -- (6.4,1.4);
                  \foreach \x in {8,...,17} \draw (\x,0) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {\small 1}; 
                  \draw[dashed, rounded corners] (7.5,-0.5) rectangle (17.5,0.5); 
                  \draw[->,thick] (6.4,0.5) -- (7.2,0.3);
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          and note that this unbundling process shows that a ten has been changed for 10 ones.
          The total number in the diagram is still 63.
          Now, we subtract 37 by taking-away 3 tens and 7 ones.
          We do this by crossing out this.
        </p>

        <image>
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                  \foreach \x in {1,...,6} \draw (\x,1) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {\small 10};
                  \foreach \x in {1,2,3} \draw (\x,0) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {\small 1}; 
                  \draw[thick] (5.6,0.6) -- (6.4,1.4);
                  \foreach \x in {8,...,17} \draw (\x,0) node[draw, circle,inner sep=2pt] {\small 1}; 
                  \draw[dashed, rounded corners] (7.5,-0.5) rectangle (17.5,0.5); 
                  \draw[->,thick] (6.4,0.5) -- (7.2,0.3);
                  \foreach \x in {1,2,3} \draw[-,thick] ({\x-0.4},0.6)--({\x+0.4},1.4);
                  \foreach \x in {8,...,14} \draw[-,thick] ({\x-0.4},-0.4)--({\x+0.4},0.4);
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          which shows 2 tens and 6 ones or 26.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Subtraction with the DLB model</title>

        <p>
          We can also use the DLB model to do subtraction.
          Much like the chip model above, we use a <em>take-away</em> interpretation and cross out the number in the subtrahend.
          The first example is <m>67-42</m> and we start with writing the DLB model for the minuend:
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \foreach \x in {0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1,1.25} \draw[thick] (\x,0)--(\x,0.8); 
                  \foreach \y in {0,0.15,0.3,0.45,0.6} \fill (1.5,{\y+0.1}) circle[radius=1.5pt]; 
                  \foreach \y in {0,0.15} \fill (1.75,{\y+0.1}) circle[radius=1.5pt]; 
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          and then we cross out 4 lines and 2 dots.
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \foreach \x in {0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1,1.25} \draw[thick] (\x,0)--(\x,0.8); 
                  \foreach \x in {0,0.25,0.5,0.75} \draw[MaterialIndigo]({\x-0.1},0.2) -- ({\x+0.1},0.3);
                  \foreach \y in {0,0.15,0.3,0.45,0.6} \fill (1.5,{\y+0.1}) circle[radius=1.5pt]; 
                  \foreach \y in {0,0.15} \fill (1.75,{\y+0.1}) circle[radius=1.5pt]; 
                  \foreach \y in {0,0.15} \draw[MaterialIndigo] (1.65,{\y+0.05}) -- (1.85,{\y+0.15}); 
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          And the remaining is 2 lines and 5 dots which represents 25.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>
  </section>